## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of least fixed-point computation in monotone boolean networks with thresholds and how to implement an efficient propagation algorithm that converges even with cycles.

## Key Concepts
- Monotone operators and least fixed points
- Threshold-based activation with in-neighbor counting
- Queue-driven BFS-like propagation
- Handling cycles via convergence to a fixpoint

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation of the propagation
function leastFixedPoint(T, edges, thresh) {
  const n = thresh.length;
  const succ = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  for (const [u,v] of edges) succ[u].push(v);
  const count = Array(n).fill(0);
  const inSet = Array(n).fill(false);
  const q = [];
  for (const i of T) { inSet[i] = true; q.push(i); }
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    for (const v of succ[u]) {
      count[v]++;
      if (!inSet[v] && count[v] >= thresh[v]) {
        inSet[v] = true;
        q.push(v);
      }
    }
  }
  return Array.from({length: n}, (_,i) => i).filter(i => inSet[i]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for weighted thresholds or non-monotone edges?
- How would you prove the result is the least fixed point containing T?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to apply Kleene three-valued logic to a realistic policy constraint problem and to design a simple backtracking propagation strategy for constraint satisfaction.

## Key Concepts
- **Kleene three-valued semantics**: Truth values T (true), F (false), and U (unknown). Operations: ¬U = U; A ∧ B = F if either is F, else U if either is U, else T; A ∨ B = T if either is T, else U if either is U, else F.
- **Implication**: A → B is equivalent to ¬A ∨ B.
- **Constraint propagation**: Systematically assign values and propagate logical consequences, backtracking when contradictions (F) arise.

## Code Example
```javascript
const values = ['T','F','U'];
function not(a) { return a === 'T' ? 'F' : a === 'F' ? 'T' : 'U'; }
function lor(a, b) { return a === 'T' || b === 'T' ? 'T' : a === 'U' || b === 'U' ? 'U' : 'F'; }
function land(a, b) { return a === 'F' || b === 'F' ? 'F' : a === 'U' || b === 'U' ? 'U' : 'T'; }
function imply(a, b) { return lor(not(a), b); }

function evaluate(vars) {
  const c1 = imply(land(vars.M1, vars.M2), vars.M3);
  const c2 = imply(lor(vars.M2, vars.M4), vars.M5);
  const c3 = imply(not(vars.M5), lor(vars.M1, vars.M4));
  return { c1, c2, c3 };
}

const example = { M1: 'U', M2: 'T', M3: 'T', M4: 'F', M5: 'U' };
console.log(evaluate(example)); // { c1: 'T', c2: 'U', c3: 'U' }
```

