## Why This Question Is Asked
This problem evaluates a candidate's ability to construct precise 3-valued assignments with exact unknown counts under Kleene semantics, along with a practical backtracking strategy for constraint satisfaction.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic semantics
- Exact unknown count in constraint satisfaction
- Propagation and backtracking in three-valued logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v) {
  return v === 'T' ? 'F' : v === 'F' ? 'T' : 'U';
}

function imp(a, b) {
  return not(a) === 'T' ? b : not(a);
}

function evaluate(expr, vars) {
  if (Array.isArray(expr)) {
    const [op, left, right] = expr;
    const l = evaluate(left, vars);
    const r = evaluate(right, vars);
    if (op === 'and') return min(l, r);
    if (op === 'or') return max(l, r);
    if (op === 'imp') return imp(l, r);
  }
  return vars[expr];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to reason about combined constraints and partial knowledge in a policy graph using three-valued logic; requires identifying a concrete satisfying assignment and developing a general propagation strategy for 3-valued logic with conjunction and implication operations.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic semantics for implication (→), conjunction (∧), and disjunction (∨)
- Propagation of unknown (U) values through mixed logical operators
- Fixpoint computation with backtracking to avoid false (F) while preserving unknowns

## Code Example

```javascript
function imp(a,b){ // a->b in Kleene logic
  const not = v => v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U';
  const or  = (x,y) => (x==='T' || y==='T') ? 'T' : (x==='U' || y==='U') ? 'U' : 'F';
  return or(not(a), b);
}

function propagate(rules, values){
  let changed;
  do{
    changed = false;
    for(const [name, expr] of rules){
      const newVal = expr(values);
      if(values[name] !== newVal){
        values[name] = newVal;
        changed = true;
      }
    }
  } while(changed);
  return values;
}
```

The algorithm iteratively propagates known values through constraints until a fixpoint is reached, ensuring no constraint evaluates to F while maintaining at least one U value.

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cross-time propagation in three-valued logic, simulates realistic policy engine scenarios, and validates backtracking capabilities with multi-step constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic
- Cross-temporal constraint satisfaction
- Backtracking with propagation and pruning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple two-period CSP checker with Kleene semantics
function kleeneNot(x) { if(x==='T') return 'F'; if(x==='F') return 'T'; return 'U'; }
function kleeneOr(a,b) { if(a==='T'||b==='T') return 'T'; if(a==='U'||b==='U') return 'U'; return 'F'; }
function kleeneAnd(a,b) { if(a==='F'||b==='F') return 'F'; if(a==='U'||b==='U') return 'U'; return 'T'; }
function kleeneImplies(a,b) { return kleeneOr(kleeneNot(a), b); }

// Check constraints across both periods
function validateConstraints(t0, t1) {
  const withinT0 = [
    kleeneImplies(kleeneOr(t0.C1, t0.C2), t0.C3),
    kleeneOr(kleeneNot(t0.C5), t0.C4),
    kleeneImplies(kleeneAnd(t0.C2, kleeneNot(t0.C3)), kleeneOr(t0.C1, t0.C5))
  ];
  const withinT1 = [
    kleeneImplies(kleeneOr(t1.C1, t1.C2), t1.C3),
    kleeneOr(kleeneNot(t1.C5), t1.C4),
    kleeneImplies(kleeneAnd(t1.C2, kleeneNot(t1.C3)), kleeneOr(t1.C1, t1.C5))
  ];
  const cross = kleeneImplies(kleeneOr(t0.C1, t1.C4), t0.C2);

  return [...withinT0, ...withinT1, cross].every(v => v !== 'F');
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason through 3-valued logic and implement a constrained solver with backtracking—a common pattern in policy/rule engines.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic with unknown (U) support
- Implication defined as ¬A ∨ B
- Backtracking propagation to avoid false outcomes

## Code Example

```javascript
function kleeneImpl(a,b){
  const not = v => v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U';
  const order = {'F':0,'U':1,'T':2};
  const na = not(a);
  const vals = [na, b];
  return vals.reduce((m,x)=> order[x] > order[m] ? x : m, 'F');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize the backtracking algorithm for larger sensor networks?
- Can you extend this approach to handle additional logical operators?
- What strategies would you use to minimize the number of U values in the final assignment?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests design and reasoning about 3-valued constraint systems with cycles, using Kleene semantics and backtracking.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic: T>U>F; not maps T<->F, U->U; and/or as min/max
- CSP propagation with non-classical values and cycles
- Depth-limited backtracking to illustrate pruning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Kleene evaluator (ASCII semantics)
const rank = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function and(a,b){ return rank[a] < rank[b] ? a : b; }
function or(a,b){ return rank[a] > rank[b] ? a : b; }
